          "claim": "The Hilbert spaces below have fixed countable dense sequences of rational complex simple functions on rational boxes or intervals. Their density follows by simple-function approximation and Lebesgue regularity; CC selects countable approximation sequences for each fixed input. Closed subspaces use their orthogonal projections of these sequences. For a real measurable multiplier $M_a$, truncation to $|a|\\le n$ proves dense domain, and testing its adjoint against all vectors supported in $|a|\\le n$ identifies the adjoint as $M_a$ on exactly $\\{h:ah\\in L^2\\}$. Thus the explicit Fourier multiplier gives self-adjoint $H_0$ on $H^2$. Hardy's inequality follows by integrating $\\operatorname{div}(x/|x|^2)|u|^2$ for smooth $u$ away from zero and using Cauchy–Schwarz: $\\|u/r\\|_2^2\\le2\\|u/r\\|_2\\|\\nabla u\\|_2$. Smooth cutoffs remove the puncture, and Sobolev density extends $\\|u/r\\|_2\\le2\\|\\nabla u\\|_2$ to $H^1$. Fourier Cauchy–Schwarz gives $\\|\\nabla u\\|_2^2\\le\\|u\\|_2\\|H_0u\\|_2$. For $z=\\pm i\\mu$, the explicit $R_0(z)=(H_0-z)^{-1}$ has $\\|R_0\\|\\le\\mu^{-1}$ and $\\|H_0R_0\\|\\le1$, hence $\\|\\gamma r^{-1}R_0(z)\\|\\le2\\gamma/\\sqrt\\mu<1$ when $\\mu>4\\gamma^2$. The factorization $C-z=(I-\\gamma r^{-1}R_0(z))(H_0-z)$, $C=H_0-\\gamma/r$ on $H^2$, gives onto-ness for both signs by the norm-convergent Neumann inverse. For $y\\in D(C^*)$ solve $(C-i\\mu)x=(C^*-i\\mu)y$; then $y-x\\in\\ker(C^*-i\\mu)=\\operatorname{ran}(C+i\\mu)^\\perp=0$. Thus $C=C^*$ on exactly $H^2$. Also $\\langle Cu,u\\rangle\\ge-\\gamma^2\\|u\\|_2^2$. This uses the explicit Hardy and Fourier calculation, rather than general Kato–Rellich.",
