          "claim": "Concrete kernel approximation: put $h_k(x,y)=e^{ik|x-y|}V(y)/(4\\pi|x-y|)$ on $B_R\\times B_R$, zero elsewhere. Its squared norm is at most $\\|V\\|_\\infty^2|B_R|(16\\pi^2)^{-1}\\int_{|z|<2R}|z|^{-2}dz<\\infty$. Thus [F1] gives boundedness, compactness and finite-rank approximation on $\\mathcal H$. More explicitly, enumerate the rational complex box-step functions restricted to $B_R$, and apply Gram-Schmidt in that order, discarding zero remainders; this uniquely specifies an orthonormal basis $(e_j)$ because the original enumeration is dense. The product basis $e_i(x)\\overline{e_j(y)}$ is complete in product $L^2$: rectangular simple functions are dense by the same kernel lemma's box approximation, and approximation of each factor approximates each finite product. Its finite square partial sums $h_{k,n}=\\sum_{i,j\\le n}a_{ij}e_i(x)\\overline{e_j(y)}$, with $a_{ij}=\\int h_k(x,y)\\overline{e_i(x)}e_j(y)dxdy$, converge in kernel norm. The associated $F_n$ has range in $\\operatorname{span}\\{e_1,\\ldots,e_n\\}$ and $\\|K_k-F_n\\|\\le\\|h_k-h_{k,n}\\|_2\\to0$. Select the least $n$ making this bound smaller than $1/2$. No maximal-family or arbitrary-basis selection occurs.",
