          "claim": "Explicit finite-rank inversion: take $F=F_n$ from step 1.1 and write $E=K_k-F$, $L=I+E$. Since $\\|E\\|<1/2$, the series $L^{-1}=\\sum_{m\\ge0}(-E)^m$ converges in operator norm, is a two-sided inverse, and has norm at most $2$. Put $T=L^{-1}F$ and $B=I+T$, so $I+K_k=LB$. The subspace $Y=\\operatorname{ran}T$ is finite dimensional. Obtain an orthonormal basis of $Y$ by Gram-Schmidt on the finite list $L^{-1}e_1,\\ldots,L^{-1}e_n$ after applying the finite coefficient matrix of $F$, discarding zero vectors; thus the orthogonal projection $P_Y$ is a finite sum of coordinate pairings. Because $T(\\mathcal H)\\subseteq Y$, $B(Y)\\subseteq Y$; injectivity of $I+K_k$ from step 2.1 makes $B|_Y$ injective and therefore bijective by finite-dimensional linear algebra. Given $h\\in\\mathcal H$, put $z=(I-P_Y)h$ and $y=(B|_Y)^{-1}(P_Yh-Tz)$. Then $B(z+y)=z+Tz+B y=h$. These finite matrix and projection operations are bounded; explicitly $\\|z+y\\|\\le[1+\\|(B|_Y)^{-1}\\|(1+\\|T\\|)]\\|h\\|$. Hence $B^{-1}$ and $(I+K_k)^{-1}=B^{-1}L^{-1}$ are bounded. If $Y=\\{0\\}$ the same formula means $B=I$. This proves full surjectivity and the lower bound without a Banach Fredholm alternative, kernel/range-chain selection or DC.",
