          "claim": "(Regular solution for a bounded measurable radial potential.) For fixed $l$ set $Q(r)=v(r)-k^2$ and seek $\\varphi_l=r^{l+1}h_l$. The equation $-\\varphi_l''+[l(l+1)/r^2+v(r)]\\varphi_l=k^2\\varphi_l$ is equivalent to $(r^{2l+2}h_l')'=r^{2l+2}Qh_l$. Its normalized regular solution is the Volterra equation $h_l(r)=1+\\frac1{2l+1}\\int_0^r t[1-(t/r)^{2l+1}]Q(t)h_l(t)\\,dt$. On every $[0,L]$, the kernel has absolute value at most $Mt/(2l+1)$ with $M=\\|Q\\|_{L^\\infty(0,L)}$, so the $n$th iterated integral is bounded by $[Mr^2/(2(2l+1))]^n/n!$. The resulting uniformly convergent series gives a continuous real solution, and applying the same estimate to differences proves uniqueness; solutions constructed on different finite intervals agree. Differentiating the integral gives $h_l'(r)=r^{-2l-2}\\int_0^r t^{2l+2}Q(t)h_l(t)\\,dt$, whence $h_l=1+O(r^2)$, $h_l'=O(r)$ as $r\\downarrow0$, and $\\varphi_l,\\varphi_l'$ are locally absolutely continuous with the radial equation valid almost everywhere. Conversely a solution with $\\varphi_l/r^{l+1}\\to1$ has no integration constant in $r^{2l+2}h_l'$ (a nonzero constant would make $h_l$ unbounded near zero), so it satisfies this Volterra equation. Near zero $h_l\\neq0$, and reduction of order gives an independent solution $\\varphi_l(r)\\int_{r_0}^r\\varphi_l(t)^{-2}\\,dt$ with leading nonzero term proportional to $r^{-l}$. Such a branch is incompatible with the local $H^2$ stationary domain: for $l\\ge1$ its reduced coefficient is not in $L^2(dr)$ near zero, while for $l=0$ its unreduced coefficient has a nonzero $1/r$ term with radial derivative proportional to $r^{-2}$ and is not locally $H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$. Thus every projected radial channel of the $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ stationary solution is a multiple of this regular $\\varphi_l$. Projection and the radial equation are justified on compact annuli by smooth approximation, the polar Laplacian identity and angular integration by parts; the angular eigenfun