          "claim": "[The exact free representation] Polar integration gives $dp=k^2\\,dk\\,d\\sigma=(k/2)\\,dE\\,d\\sigma$. Consequently $\\|F_0g\\|_{\\mathcal K}^2=\\int|\\widehat g(p)|^2dp=\\|g\\|_2^2$. Conversely a measurable $a(E,\\omega)$ defines $\\widehat g(k\\omega)=\\sqrt{2/k}\\,a(k^2,\\omega)$, which has the required $L^2(dp)$ norm; Fourier inversion gives its unique preimage. Thus $F_0$ is onto and intertwines $H_0$ with $M_E$. On a bounded spatial support, the integral defining $Z_0(E)$ is bounded into $\\mathfrak h$ by Cauchy-Schwarz, uniformly on compact positive $E$ intervals, and depends continuously in operator norm on $E$ by bounded-support differentiation of the exponential. Its coefficient is $\\sqrt{k/2}(2\\pi)^{-3/2}=\\sqrt k/(4\\pi^{3/2})$.",
