          "claim": "[Local resolvent boundary limits] On bounded balls the kernel $e^{i\\sqrt z|x-y|}/(4\\pi|x-y|)$, with the upper-half-plane square-root branch, converges in Hilbert-Schmidt norm to the outgoing kernel as $z=E+i\\varepsilon\\to E+i0$, locally uniformly for $E>0$: the dominating squared singularity $C|x-y|^{-2}$ is integrable in three dimensions. Multiplication by bounded $V$ preserves this convergence. Invertibility at each real $E$ is [F2]; the Neumann perturbation identity for a bounded inverse gives continuous inverses on a neighborhood of each such $E$, and a finite cover of a compact positive interval gives a uniform inverse bound. For a compact source $g$, enlarge the ball to contain its support and use the resolvent equation to obtain $\\chi_BR_H(z)g=(I+K_z)^{-1}\\chi_BR_0(z)g$. Therefore these local resolvents converge in operator norm on compact-source/local-output spaces. The lower limits follow by complex conjugation and have the adjoint reciprocity $\\bigl(\\chi_BR_H(E+i0)\\chi_{B'}\\bigr)^*=\\chi_{B'}R_H(E-i0)\\chi_B$. This proves the local norm boundary construction and its uniform energy control; no global unweighted trace bound is asserted.",
