          "claim": "[Radial regular and outgoing solutions] For the radial specialization (d) only, assume radial $V$. For each fixed $l,k$ the regular solution is constructed by the Volterra equation $\\varphi_l(r)=r^{l+1}+\\int_0^rG_l(r,s)(V(s)-k^2)\\varphi_l(s)ds$, where $G_l(r,s)=[r^{l+1}s^{-l}-r^{-l}s^{l+1}]/(2l+1)$. Dividing by $r^{l+1}$ bounds the normalized kernel by $s/(2l+1)$. On $[0,L]$, successive iterates of the error are bounded by $C^n L^{2n}/(2^n n!)$, proving convergence, uniqueness, the stated origin normalization and local energy continuity. Differentiation gives the radial equation, and for real energy the solution is real. Outside the support, an exact outgoing solution is $e^{i(kr-l\\pi/2)}\\sum_{j=0}^l i^j(l+j)!/[j!(l-j)!(2kr)^j]$; direct coefficient substitution verifies the free radial equation and outgoing normalization. Continue it inward on compact intervals away from zero using the usual integral-equation Picard series, whose factorial estimates need only countable iteration. The Wronskian is constant. If it vanished, the nonzero real regular solution would be proportional to the outgoing solution outside the support. Its imaginary radial current is zero, whereas the outgoing current is $k$ times the squared proportionality constant; this forces that constant to vanish and contradicts the origin normalization. Thus $W_l\\ne0$.",
