          "claim": "[The strong Abel limit, including bound states] Put $W(t)=e^{itH_0}e^{-itH}$. For $g=\\Omega_+h$, $\\|W(t)g-h\\|=\\|\\Omega_+h-W(t)^*h\\|\\to0$ by the defining wave limit. Hence $W(t)\\to\\Omega_+^*$ strongly on $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)$. Its Abel average $A_\\varepsilon^+=\\varepsilon\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\varepsilon t}W(t)dt$ has the same limit there: split the integral at a time after which the norm error is below a prescribed tolerance; the fixed finite initial interval has weight tending to zero. If $Hg=\\lambda g$, the average instead equals $\\varepsilon(\\varepsilon-i(H_0-\\lambda))^{-1}g$ and tends to zero, since its squared spectral multiplier is bounded by one and tends to zero off the singleton $\\{\\lambda\\}$, which has zero free spectral measure. Finite sums of point eigenvectors are dense in the pure point subspace; all averages have norm at most one, so the limit is zero on that whole subspace. The density uses only the countable atomic decomposition on this separable Hilbert space. By [F1] there is no singular continuous remainder. Thus $A_\\varepsilon^+\\to\\Omega_+^*$ strongly on all $L^2$. Reversing time gives $A_\\varepsilon^-\\to\\Omega_-^*$, with $A_\\varepsilon^-=\\varepsilon\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\varepsilon t}W(-t)dt$.",
