          "step": "2.3",
          "claim": "[The angular normalization, with its derivation] The explicit associated-polynomial harmonics are restrictions of homogeneous harmonic polynomials of degree $l$: substitute their Rodrigues formulas into $r^lY_{lm}(x/r)$ and the Cartesian Laplacian to check polynomiality and harmonicity. This space has dimension $2l+1$. Indeed the Laplacian from degree $l$ polynomials onto degree $l-2$ polynomials is surjective: twice integrating a target polynomial in $x_3$ gives an operator $J$ with $\\partial_3^2Jq=q$, and the finite sum $\\sum_n(-J(\\partial_1^2+\\partial_2^2))^nJq$ cancels the other two second derivatives; the sum terminates because their degree in $x_1,x_2$ drops. Subtract the two polynomial-space dimensions. The $2l+1$ orthonormal $Y_{lm}$ therefore span this space, which is invariant under orthogonal rotations. Its projection kernel $K_l(\\omega,d)=\\sum_mY_{lm}(\\omega)\\overline{Y_{lm}(d)}$ is rotation invariant. At the north pole only $m=0$ survives, with $Y_{l0}=\\sqrt{(2l+1)/(4\\pi)}P_l$ and $P_l(1)=1$; hence $K_l(\\omega,d)=(2l+1)P_l(\\omega\\cdot d)/(4\\pi)$ after rotation. Define $j_l(z)=z^l\\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}(1-t^2)^ldt/(2^{l+1}l!)$. Integrating Rodrigues' formula by parts $l$ times gives $\\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}P_l(t)dt=2i^lj_l(z)$. Endpoint integration by parts in the polynomial $P_l$ gives $j_l(z)=\\sin(z-l\\pi/2)/z+O(z^{-2})$; the power series at zero gives $j_l(z)\\sim z^l/(2l+1)!!$. Differentiating the integral and integrating its $t$ derivative verifies $z^2j_l''+2zj_l'+(z^2-l(l+1))j_l=0$. Project the zonal exponential at the north pole onto degree $l$: integration in the azimuth kills $m\\ne0$ and the displayed Legendre integral fixes its $m=0$ coefficient. Rotate the result and use $K_l$ to obtain $\\int_{S^2}e^{iz\\omega\\cdot d}Y_{lm}(d)d\\sigma(d)=4\\pi i^lj_l(z)Y_{lm}(\\omega)$. Thus its factor, phase and origin/free asymptotics have all been computed. Angular channel projections commute with the radial Hamiltonian: on smooth compact spatial tests this follows from the polar Laplacian and this finite harmonic projection; Taylor expansion at the origin ensures the projected tests remain smooth there. The projections preserve the $H^2$ domain by density and commutation with the free graph norm, so the regular radial reduction is legitimate.",
