          "claim": "[The regular radial spectral transform is genuinely onto] The angular decomposition reduces the radial potential and free Laplacian to the countably many reduced radial problems, with the regular origin boundary inherited from $H^2(\\mathbb R^3)$. The local outgoing radial Green kernel is $-\\varphi_l(r_<)f_l(r_>)/W_l$: its derivative jump is $-1$, and its origin and outgoing conditions characterize it uniquely. Since $\\varphi_l$ is real and $\\varphi_l=c f_l+d\\overline f_l$, $d=W_l/(2ik)$ and $c=-\\overline W_l/(2ik)=\\overline d$. Therefore the difference of the outgoing and incoming Green kernels is $2ik\\varphi_l(r)\\varphi_l(s)/|W_l|^2$. The same explicit Poisson spectral argument as step 4.1 yields density $\\rho_l\\varphi_l(r)\\varphi_l(s)$, with $\\rho_l=k/(\\pi|W_l|^2)$. Thus $T_l$ initially has squared norm $\\|P_{\\mathrm{ac}}g\\|^2$ on compact sources and extends to an isometry on the absolutely continuous radial subspace. For an energy set $A$, polarization of the density formula says that $T_lP_H(A)g$ is the orthogonal range projection of $1_AT_lg$; their norms are equal because both squared norms equal the spectral measure of $g$ on $A$. Hence $1_AT_lg$ is already in the range and $T_lP_H(A)g=1_AT_lg$. Its range is therefore closed and reducing; the scalar case of step 7.1 says its range projection is multiplication by $1_A$ for a measurable energy set $A$. Fix a countable dense list of compact radial step functions. At each positive energy not all their integrals against the nonzero continuous local function $\\sqrt{\\rho_l}\\varphi_l$ vanish. Their transformed representatives are continuous in energy locally. Since each lies in the range, all vanish a.e. on the complement of $A$; the countable union shows that complement is null. This proves onto, not merely an isometric kernel formula. The regular origin condition used here follows from the spherical-harmonic radial decomposition: the other local solution has reduced leading term $r^{-l}$, which is not locally $L^2$ for $l\\ge1$; for $l=0$ its three-dimensional $1/r$ term is excluded by $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ and its delta Laplacian.",
