          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the Lebesgue-measure interfaces. Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space, let $a<b$, and let $f:[a,b]\\to X$ be continuous. Differentiation uses the underlying real structure. Then $G(t):=\\int_a^t f(s)\\,ds$ is differentiable on $(a,b)$ and has the corresponding one-sided derivatives at $a,b$, with $G'(t)=f(t)$, and this derivative extends continuously to $[a,b]$. Consequently, if $\\varphi:[a,b]\\to X$ is continuous, differentiable on $(a,b)$ with $\\varphi'$ continuous on $(a,b)$ and extendable to a continuous $X$-valued function on $[a,b]$, then $\\int_a^b\\varphi'(s)\\,ds=\\varphi(b)-\\varphi(a)$. The Bochner integral here is the one of [[def-bochner-integrable-function]]; the identities also hold for continuous curves on $[0,\\infty)$ restricted to compact subintervals.",
